   `#v65-cart-shipping-details select, #v65-cart-shipping-details input
   { width: 150px !important }` using an ID selector, which beats the
   existing sitewide `html.shoppingcart select { width:100% !important }`
   rule on specificity (1 ID > 1 class) despite both carrying !important.
   Matching the ID in this override wins the tie via source order (this
   rule is later in the file). Confirmed live: select right edge moved
   from 428px to 388px (within the 393px test budget) after this. Note:
   the selects end up quite narrow (~90px) as a result, which truncates
   their own displayed text ("United Sta...") — that's the native
   <select> control showing its selected option in a tight box, not a
   viewport overflow; the full option list is still reachable by tapping
   it open. A real fix for the truncation itself would mean redesigning
   the label+input layout (stacking labels above inputs instead of
